Energy · October 2, 2026
G7 will release 100 million barrels over four months and does not say how much is beyond its March commitments
The leaders' statement promises a substantial diesel release in the first 20 days and gives no number for it. Of the 400 million barrels International Energy Agency members pledged in March, about 325 million had been released.
100 million barrels coordinated release beginning immediately
4 months the period named for that release
20 days named for the diesel release
400 million barrels March coordinated release
What happened
On October 2, 2026, the G7 leaders agreed to a coordinated release through the International Energy Agency of 100 million barrels over four months, beginning immediately, with a substantial diesel share in the first 20 days and no number for that diesel, and they said they will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible, and will refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries. The statement sets that release "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled," which means the G7 did not say how much of the 100 million is new beyond the March commitments.
Weekly U.S. retail diesel was 6.382 dollars a gallon on September 28. On March 2 it was 3.897. The Independent says prices have rocketed in recent months because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's war in Ukraine. The G7 says the measures are meant to "shield households and businesses from price shocks", and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage. The European Commission's Energy Union task force said EU diesel supply remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high.
The French presidency's readout puts the ceiling at up to 100 million barrels within four months. The readout's own wording places refined products, diesel, and crude oil under that ceiling.
In March, International Energy Agency members pledged 400 million barrels; by October 2 around 325 million of that March action had been released. On September 29 the United States offered an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude, and the companies that take the barrels return the borrowed barrels later with additional premium barrels, continuing the 172 million barrels it announced in March. On September 30 President Donald Trump was still thinking about banning diesel exports. On October 1 Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available. On October 2 the leaders agreed to a coordinated release of 100 million barrels over four months, with a substantial diesel share in the first 20 days, and hours later President Trump said he will not authorize an export ban on diesel. The joint statement commits the seven countries to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between themselves. The March release was still being carried out, the October statement set a 100 million barrel four-month timetable with an early diesel share and did not say how much of it is new beyond March, and the United States dropped the export-ban threat.
On October 5, Japanese Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara told a press conference, "Japan has been releasing oil from reserves for some time now and there are no plans for a further release at this stage." Reuters reported that in March Japan said it would release about 80 million barrels of oil from its reserves as part of the coordinated agreement.
The G7 statement, the French readout and the International Energy Agency's account of the meeting do not say whether any October barrel has been delivered.
The International Energy Agency said in March that the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products. A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells. The October decision also says the countries will coordinate maintenance schedules across their refineries to prevent simultaneous shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization where feasible, and it encourages engagement with countries that hold significant refining capacity to boost production of refined products, particularly diesel. The International Energy Agency's September report says net exports of diesel and gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390,000 barrels a day in August, just over a quarter of levels before the war in the Middle East, and that exports from the Gulf and Russia together were 1.6 million barrels a day lower than in February. The French presidency's readout says volumes moving through Hormuz, the Strait of Hormuz where the joint statement calls for the restoration of navigational rights, and through the Yanbu canal in the Red Sea have been rising in recent days. On September 30, Energy Secretary Chris Wright said diesel supplies are "tight" because conflicts involving Russia and the Middle East have cut exports, and that American refiners are running at record highs.
The catch
A diesel release, and no number for it
President Donald Trump posted that Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil and that the process will begin immediately. Before the statement, Reuters reported that the United States had demanded 100 million barrels of diesel from large European countries within 20 days. The same report said European Union countries that day discussed a French proposal for 50 million barrels of diesel from Europe and 50 million barrels of crude oil from International Energy Agency members. Those were positions before the agreement. The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks". The joint statement gives no diesel number, names no country that will supply it, and reports nothing delivered. On October 5 the White House said President Trump had negotiated with Europe to release 100 million barrels of refined diesel from its strategic reserves within the next four months, against that joint text, which promises only a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days and names no amount, and against the French readout, which describes refined products, diesel, and crude. None of the White House claim, the joint text, or the French readout is an allocation or a record of barrels delivered.
An X post said "100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States," and attributed that to Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins. A Scripps article publishes excerpts of an interview with Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins, and in them she says the administration would do everything it could in the short term.
In the interview's closed captions she says, "100 million barrels that will be headed this way very, very soon. fact, I think they’re moving out right now." She does not call the barrels diesel in that passage. That post turns her I think into a flat claim and adds diesel. In a later turn she says she is the secretary of agriculture, not energy, and that she is not in any of these conversations.
Where this sits
Oil reserve decisions March to October 2026.
Date Decision
Mar 11 400 million barrels pledged International Energy Agency members pledge a release. The United States says it will release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.
Sep 29 Exchange of up to 40 million barrels Participating companies return the borrowed barrels later with extra barrels. This continues the March release. Several European countries had released only a fraction of the crude and products they pledged.
Sep 30 A diesel export ban still under discussion President Trump said he was thinking about banning diesel exports.
Oct 1 Bessent asks Europe to move existing commitments He says European partners should accelerate delivery on existing commitments and make additional supplies available.
Oct 2 100 million barrels over four months The statement takes fulfilled commitments into account, includes a substantial diesel release in the first 20 days, and gives no number for the diesel. Trump says he will not authorize an export ban.
Oct 5 Japan plans no further release Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara says Japan has been releasing oil from reserves and has no plans for a further release at this stage.
Oct 5 An order on dyed-diesel tax The order tells the Treasury secretary to defer the highway excise tax on dyed diesel through December 31 if he determines the relief is authorized. Within five days he is to direct the Internal Revenue Service to announce it will not impose a penalty. It is not a reserve delivery.
