The export ban
On October 1, Reuters reported that the United States had asked the European Union to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months. Politico reported that one European diplomat characterized the White House’s demands for a release against the threat of a U.S. export ban explicitly as “blackmail.”
On October 2 President Trump said he will not authorize an export ban on diesel. He added, "It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing." The National reported that President Macron said Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel.
European Commission spokeswoman Anna-Kaisa Itkonen said, "We fully reject any ban on diesel."
Ursula von der Leyen, European Commission president · October 2, 2026 · X post, via RTÉ
We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks.
RTÉ
What comes next
On October 2 the G7 said it would meet again through the International Energy Agency in the coming days to discuss more diesel releases if needed. It invites the agency to monitor the impact of the measures and whether the commitments are carried out. It says a follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days and should "include actionable recommendations to enhance future responses, including stocks replenishment." On October 5 President Trump signed an order saying restricted global diesel supply has led to rising prices and telling the Treasury secretary to defer the excise tax incurred from October 5 through December 31, 2026, if he determines the relief is authorized. Within five days the secretary is to direct the Internal Revenue Service to announce it will not impose a penalty when dyed diesel is used on the highway in that period. The White House fact sheet says the order allows off-road dyed diesel for highway use, and the measure is a tax and penalty step on U.S. fuel, not a reserve delivery. Reuters reported that it was uncertain how much of the new release would come from what remains from the 400 million barrels of oil that the 32 countries that are members of the International Energy Agency agreed to release in March.
Participating companies will return the barrels they borrow in the September 29 exchange later with extra barrels. Bids are due October 6. Solicitations before the September 29 exchange awarded more than 133 million barrels, and deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December.
What we do not know yet
How much of the 100 million barrels is beyond the March pledge, and which countries will supply it, the joint record does not say. The split between diesel and crude is unstated in that joint record and is asserted by the White House, which on October 5 said the release is 100 million barrels of refined diesel from Europe's strategic reserves within four months.
